    "result_text": "Tests green. I agree with the finding and addressed it.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI accepted the finding \u2014 it correctly points out that the per-city count was duplicated against the codebase's own established pattern (`BusinessDays.createdOn` shared by the daily-limit check and `FlagOwnerBulkSignup`). The city case now follows the same shape:\n\n1. **New `OwnerCities` helper** (mirroring `BusinessDays`) \u2014 owns both:\n   - `countInCity(ownerRepository, city)` \u2014 the single source of truth for the per-city count.\n   - `public static final int CITY_CAPACITY = 50` \u2014 the one copy of the limit.\n\n2. **`CheckOwnerCityCapacity`** \u2014 now rejects at `>= OwnerCities.CITY_CAPACITY` over `OwnerCities.countInCity(...)`; dropped its private `CITY_CAPACITY` constant and the inlined query.\n\n3. **`FlagOwnerCapacityWarning`** \u2014 now flags at `>= WARNING_THRESHOLD && < OwnerCities.CITY_CAPACITY` over the same shared count; keeps only its own `WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40`.\n\nThis settles the null-handling divergence in one place too: the shared helper uses the `city != null && city.equals(...)` guard, so the reject and warning counts are now defined identically and can't drift. The hard limit, the warning band, and the count definition all live in `OwnerCities`.\n\nThe full suite (`AcceptanceTest`, including the approaching-capacity case) is green: `BUILD SUCCESS`, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0.",
